 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "vesanae-libidinis",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Moral Diction",
   "quote": "vesanae libidinis et invidiae noxiae stimulis agitata",
   "trans": "driven by the goads of mad lust and harmful jealousy",
   "body": "The sister is condemned in her every epithet: <em>vesana libido</em> (\"frenzied lust\") and <em>invidia noxia</em> (\"ruinous envy\") are the <em>stimuli</em> (\"goads\") that <em>drive</em> her. The animal image of being goaded marks her as a creature of base passions.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The loaded phrases <em>vesana libido</em> and <em>invidia noxia</em>, with the goad-metaphor <em>stimulis agitata</em>, brand the sister as driven by sub-human passions, preparing us to feel no pity at her fate.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "caeca-spe",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Irony",
   "quote": "caeca spe tamen inhians",
   "trans": "gaping, nevertheless, in blind hope",
   "body": "<em>Caeca spes</em> (\"blind hope\") is the perfect phrase for her delusion: she gazes hungrily (<em>inhians</em>) for a Zephyr that will not come. The adverse wind blows and yet she leaps — hope has blinded her to the obvious.",
   "exam": "<strong>In an exam:</strong> \"The oxymoron-like <em>caeca spe ... inhians</em> captures the sister’s fatal self-deception: ‘blind’ with greedy hope, she ignores the contrary wind and the absent god, gaping after a rescue that will never come.\"",
   "line": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "accipe-dominam",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Hubris",
   "quote": "accipe me, Cupido, dignam te coniugem ... suscipe dominam",
   "trans": "Take me, Cupid, a wife worthy of you ... receive your mistress",
   "body": "Her last words reek of arrogance: she names herself a <em>coniunx digna</em> (\"a worthy wife\") and a <em>domina</em> (\"mistress\"). The pompous command to Cupid and Zephyr — neither of whom is listening — makes her self-importance the immediate cause of her ruin.",
